## The short answer

Because Kafka is not a queue. It is a **log**: a list of records you can only add to at the end. Kafka calls it a **distributed, append-only commit log**. Producers add records to the end. Consumers **read** them.

**Consuming is a read, not a removal.** Each **consumer group** (a team of readers, like "inventory") only remembers **how far it has read**. That position is called its **offset**, like a bookmark. The group saves it in a special internal topic called `__consumer_offsets`. The broker (the Kafka server) never tracks "this consumer finished this message". So nothing ever triggers a delete.

So when is data deleted? By a **retention policy**, no matter who has read it:
- after a **time limit** (`retention.ms`, **7 days by default**),
- after a **size limit** (`retention.bytes`),
- or, for **compacted** topics, when a newer value for the same key replaces an older one.

What this buys you:
- **Speed and simplicity**: the disk is read and written in order (sequential I/O). There is no per-message bookkeeping.
- **Fan-out**: many teams read the same stream at their own pace. Adding a new team costs the producer nothing.
- **Replay**: rewind a bookmark to fix a bug, fill a new service with history, or rebuild a cache or search index.
- **Slow or crashed readers do not hurt anyone**: a reader that is down for hours just catches up.
- **A durable history** for audit and event sourcing, and **the reader picks its delivery guarantee**.

The price: **storage**, readers that must be **idempotent** (safe to run twice, because replays create duplicates), harder **deletion of personal data**, **lag monitoring**, and **no built-in retry or dead-letter queue** per message.

## The intuition

Shreya orders a phone on a Flipkart-like app. One event, "order 101 placed", goes into Kafka. Four teams need it: inventory, notifications, fraud and analytics.

A classic **queue** (RabbitMQ, SQS) is like a **tray of letters**. A clerk takes one letter, deals with it and shreds it. If three departments need it, the post room must make three copies up front.

Kafka is like a **ship's logbook** or a **newspaper archive**. Entries are written in order. Nobody tears out a page after reading it. Every reader keeps a **bookmark**.
- Finance has its bookmark on page 9,400.
- Analytics reads slowly, so its bookmark is on page 8,100.
- A new auditor starts at page 1.

Nobody's reading affects anybody else. Old volumes are thrown away **on a schedule**, say after a week, never because someone read them.

Words you need (a **topic** is a named stream; it is split into **partitions**, and each partition is an ordered log stored on a **broker**):
- **Offset**: the position number of a record in a partition: 0, 1, 2 and so on. It is the bookmark.
- **Consumer group**: a set of reader programs that share the work of reading a topic. Each group has its own bookmarks.
- **Commit**: a group saving "I have finished up to offset N".
- **Retention**: the rule that decides when old data is deleted.
- **Segment**: one file on disk holding a run of records from a partition. It is the unit of deletion.
- **Log compaction**: a clean-up mode that keeps only the latest record for each key.
- **Consumer lag**: how far a group is behind. It is the newest offset minus the group's bookmark.

## How it actually works

### 1. Reading does not change the log

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  P[Order service] --> L[order_events log]
  L --> G1[Inventory at 8995]
  L --> G2[Notifications at 8990]
  L --> G3[Analytics at 7200]
  L --> G4[Fraud at 8999]
  G1 --> O[Bookmarks topic]
  G3 --> O
```

A consumer asks the broker: "give me partition 0 from offset 8995, up to 1 MB". The broker sends bytes from its files and **changes nothing**.

After processing, the consumer commits offset 9000. That is the *next* offset it wants. The commit is just a small record in `__consumer_offsets`. That internal topic is compacted and keyed by group, topic and partition.

